      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e30206ee_92dca04d",
        "filename": "common/mkbp_event.c",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1113904
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-03-08T22:18:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see what you\u0027re talking about:\n\nparent_function() {\n    interrupt_disable;\n    do_something_1();\n    our_function();\n    do_something_2();\n    interrupt_enable();\n}\n\nour_function() {\n    interrupt_disable;\n    do_something_3();\n    do_something_4();\n    interrupt_enable();\n}\n\nThe issue is that do_something_2() will run with interrupts re-enabled because our_function called interrupt_enable() too early.\n\nThe way to fix it would be:\nour_function() {\n    saved_interrupt_state \u003d get_interrupt_state();\n    //A\n    interrupt_disable;\n    do_something_3();\n    do_something_4();\n    //interrupt_enable();\n    set_interrupt_state(saved_interrupt_state);\n}\n\nI\u0027ve seen this this pattern in a few places in the code. We have 2 issues though:\n* We have no api to do get_interrupt_state() in stm32. We have it in a couple of other chips (looks like only nds32/it83xx)\n* Wouldn\u0027t there be a race condition at point A, what if something interrupts in there and changes the state of the interrupts. When we put it back we\u0027ll put the old state, not the new one. I guess that\u0027s unlikely.\n\nNote, our stm32 code has a ton of other cases where this happens. I\u0027m not looking forward to yak shave on this. Created b/74401691",
      "parentUuid": "7c30a93b_8413d423",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 58,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "14231a1d20704dac5694b1bb491cc06a665642b1",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
